'''Lardon Chase, the Holies and Lough Down''' is a [[National Trust]] countryside property in the [[England|English]] county of [[Berkshire]]. It is situated on the edge of the [[Berkshire Downs]] above the village of [[Streatley, Berkshire|Streatley]] and overlooking the [[Goring Gap]]. The property comprises an outstanding area of 27 [[hectare]]s (67 [[acre]]s) of [[downland]] and [[woodland]] with many attractive walks and views. [[Lardon Chase]], and a part of the Holies known as [[Holies Down]], are also designated as [[Sites of Special Scientific Interest]]. The property lies within the [[North Wessex Downs AONB|North Wessex Downs Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty]], and in an area known for the  presence of several [[Neolithic]] and [[Iron Age]] forts.<ref name=tsolcount>{{cite web | title = Thames & Solent - Places to Visit - Countryside | publisher = National Trust | accessdate = 2007-11-04 | url = http://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/main/w-global/w-localtoyou/w-thames_solent/w-thames_solent-countryside/w-thames_solent-places-countryside-list.htm#lardon_chase | archivedate = 2007-11-07 | url-status = dead | archiveurl = https://web.archive.org/web/20071107052109/http://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/main/w-global/w-localtoyou/w-thames_solent/w-thames_solent-countryside/w-thames_solent-places-countryside-list.htm#lardon_chase }}</ref><ref name=sssilc>{{cite web | title = SSSI Information - Lardon Chase | url = https://designatedsites.naturalengland.org.uk/SiteDetail.aspx?SiteCode=S1003854&SiteName=chase&countyCode=&responsiblePerson= | publisher = Natural England | accessdate = 2016-01-02 | archivedate=2016-02-02 | url-status = live | archiveurl = https://web.archive.org/web/20160202112031/https://designatedsites.naturalengland.org.uk/SiteDetail.aspx?SiteCode=S1003854&SiteName=chase&countyCode=&responsiblePerson=}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://magic.defra.gov.uk/MagicMap.aspx?startTopic=Designations&activelayer=sssiIndex&query=HYPERLINK%3D%271003854%27 |title=Magic Map Application - Lardon Chase SSSI |publisher=Magic.defra.gov.uk |date= |accessdate=2017-03-19}}</ref><ref name=sssihd>{{cite web | title = SSSI Information - Holies Down | url = https://designatedsites.naturalengland.org.uk/SiteDetail.aspx?SiteCode=S1005585&SiteName=&countyCode=&responsiblePerson=DES%20SUSSEX | publisher = Natural England | accessdate = 2016-01-02 | archivedate=2016-02-02 | url-status = live | archiveurl = https://web.archive.org/web/20160202112408/https://designatedsites.naturalengland.org.uk/SiteDetail.aspx?SiteCode=S1005585&SiteName=&countyCode=&responsiblePerson=DES%20SUSSEX}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://magic.defra.gov.uk/MagicMap.aspx?startTopic=Designations&activelayer=sssiIndex&query=HYPERLINK%3D%271005585%27 |title=Magic Map Application - Holies Down SSSI |publisher=Magic.defra.gov.uk |date= |accessdate=2017-03-19}}</ref>

Lardon Chase and Lough Down comprise a spur of downland which lies to the west of Goring and Streatley.  Since it was acquired by the National Trust, the land has been managed by a mixture of scrub clearance and cattle grazing, so as to encourage the growth of chalk-loving plants. The Holies is a woodland fringed grassy [[Combe|coombe]] which is separated from Lardon Chase and Lough Down by the [[B4009 road]] from Streatley to [[Newbury, Berkshire|Newbury]]. Before acquisition by the National Trust, the Holies was used for [[motocross|motorbike scrambling]] and [[grass|turf]] stripping, resulting in considerable damage to the ground. The National Trust is encouraging the natural recolonisation of the area by chalk-loving plant species.<ref name=tsolcount/><ref name=nelc>{{cite web | url = http://www.sssi.naturalengland.org.uk/citation/citation_photo/1003854.pdf | title = Lardon Chase | publisher = Natural England | accessdate = 2016-02-02 | archivedate = 2016-02-02 | url-status = live | archiveurl = https://web.archive.org/web/20160202155758/http://www.sssi.naturalengland.org.uk/citation/citation_photo/1003854.pdf}}</ref> There are three [[turf maze]]s in the Holies.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/travel/2012/may/12/the-holies-berkshire-walk|title=The Holies, Berkshire &#124; Great British walks|date=11 May 2012}}</ref>

The slopes of the property form one of the largest remaining areas of [[Calcareous grassland|chalk grassland]] in the county and support a wide range of flowers and butterflies. Plants found in the grassland include [[autumn gentian]], [[clustered bellflower]], [[blue fleabane]], [[vervain]], [[common rock-rose]], [[horseshoe vetch]], [[kidney vetch]], [[marjoram]], [[yellow-wort]], [[purging flax]], [[wild carrot]], [[chalk milkwort]], [[stemless thistle]], [[eyebright]], [[mouse-ear hawkweed]], [[salad burnet]], [[ragwort]], [[wild thyme]], [[hairy violet]], [[squinancywort]], [[hawkweed ox-tongue]], [[common spotted orchid]] and [[pyramidal orchid]]. Scattered bands of [[Crataegus|hawthorn scrub]] provide shelter for insect life and several butterfly species can be found, including [[chalkhill blue]], [[Melanargia galathea|marbled white]], [[adonis blue]], [[grizzled skipper]] and [[dingy skipper]].<ref name=tsolcount/><ref name=nelc/><ref name=nehd>{{cite web | url = http://www.sssi.naturalengland.org.uk/citation/citation_photo/1005585.pdf | title = Holies Down | publisher = Natural England | accessdate = 2016-02-03 | archivedate = 2016-02-03 | url-status = live | archiveurl = https://web.archive.org/web/20160203132220/http://www.sssi.naturalengland.org.uk/citation/citation_photo/1005585.pdf}}</ref>

The property is accessible from a car park located on the B4009, or by steeply climbing footpaths from Streatley village. The Holies is also accessible (on foot only) from the [[A329 road]] south of Streatley, whilst Lough Down can be accessed from the ancient track of [[The Ridgeway]] on its northern boundary. Access is free, and available at all times. The property adjoins the [[Goring & Streatley Golf Club]].

